; snes_pad.loom
; A Super Nintendo (SNES / Super Famicom) game controller: the pad side of
; the controller port. LATCH loads the button word, each CLOCK rise shifts
; the next button onto DATA, active low; the buttons come from the host.
;
; Written on 2026-11-07, after the hardware was complete (D-032), as a
; protocol Loom was not designed for (D-035): the SNES controller port is
; in no Loom design document. Nintendo published no specification; every
; figure comes from public secondary sources, cited in
; tools/protomodels/snes_pad.py. The program uses:
;   - the bit engine's shift register in manual mode (SHO, SEMANTICS 6.9),
;     built for the synchronous serial programs, as the pad's 4021-type
;     shift register: LDSR is the parallel load, SHO the shift, and the
;     zero shifted in behind the 16 bits is the 4021's grounded serial
;     input (after 16 clocks DATA stays low);
;   - the timed waits (WAITP ... T, SEMANTICS 6.4), built for the
;     receivers' timeouts: every LATCH and CLOCK level is waited for with
;     a deadline, so a console that stops in the middle cannot hang it.
; What the firmware does that no hardware does: the latch-and-shift
; sequence of the 4021 following the console's lines, the button word
; from the host, and the recovery from a cut-short read. Weaker than a
; real pad: a 4021 changes DATA a few tens of ns after the CLOCK rise;
; this program changes it 7 to 15 clocks (0.14 to 0.3 us at 50 MHz) after
; it (the two-flop synchroniser, the 4-clock slot grid, one slot for the
; SHO, the output register). The console samples at the next fall, a
; half period later (6 us in the sources, 0.5 us at the fastest), so it
; is enough down to a clock half of about 0.32 us. The latch's parallel
; load happens once, at the rise: a host word that arrives while LATCH is
; high is used from the next latch.
;
; Pins: LATCH = IN1, CLOCK = IN2, DATA = OUT2 (push-pull, low at start).
; With snes_host.loom on thread 0 the board wires are OUT0 to IN1, OUT1 to
; IN2 and OUT2 to IN0.
;
; Host words (INQ[1]): the buttons, 1 = pressed, in the layout of
; snes_host.loom's reply (bit 15 B, the first bit sent, down to bit 4 R;
; bits 3:0 the last four bits, 0 for a standard pad). The pad starts with
; nothing pressed. No reply.
;
; Timing: the program sets its own tick, TK = 50 clocks, 1 us at 50 MHz.
; Idle, it looks for LATCH high and for a host word in PIECE = 16 us
; pieces. After the latch every level waits CTO = 100 us at most (the
; sources' whole read is about 200 us, one clock half 6 us); a timeout
; drops the read and goes back to idle, as a console that stops early
; would leave a real pad (the next latch reloads it). A LATCH held high
; for longer than CTO reloads the word and waits again.
;
; Registers: r1 the word as it goes on the wire (active low), r2 the
; clock rises still to follow, r7 .csr scratch.
;
; Deadline proof: no pair. The program keeps no schedule of its own; its
; deadlines are only the timeouts of its waits. One .bounded: the POP,
; guarded by WAITB INQ_NE, T.
;
; Memory: 32 words from 0x080, thread 1's quarter (.imem 512).
;
        .imem   512                     ; the macro build: quarters of 128 words
        .thread 1
        .pins   LAT = IN1, CLK = IN2, DATA = OUT2
        .equ    TK = 50                 ; ticks of 1 us at 50 MHz
        .equ    PIECE = 16              ; idle: a look at INQ every 16 us
        .equ    CTO = 100               ; a level not seen in 100 us: give up

start:  SETP    DATA, 0
        .csr    BE_PINS, pin(DATA)      ; SHO drives DATA (no SHI)
        .csr    BE_CFG, 2               ; DIR = 1: MSB first, bit 15 (B) first
        .csr    TICK_INT, TK
        MOV16   r1, 0xFFFF              ; nothing pressed: all high on the wire

idle:   SETD    PIECE
        WAITP   LAT, 1, T
        BNT     latch
        WAITB   INQ_NE, T               ; a test: TD was reached
        BT      idle
        .bounded "guarded by the WAITB INQ_NE above; only this thread pops INQ[1]"
        POP     r1
        NOT     r1, r1                  ; 1 = pressed -> low on the wire
        JMP     idle

latch:  LDSR    r1                      ; the parallel load
        SHO                             ; the first bit (B) on DATA
        LDI     r2, 16                  ; 15 more bits, then the 0 behind them
        SETD    CTO
        WAITP   LAT, 0, T
        BT      latch                   ; held high: load again
bit:    SETD    CTO
        WAITP   CLK, 0, T
        BT      idle                    ; the read was cut short
        WAITP   CLK, 1, T
        BT      idle
        SHO                             ; the rise shifts the next bit out
        DJNZ    r2, bit
        JMP     idle
